# SHA-256 of every committed golden vector in this workspace.
#
# WHAT: the 46 .bin files under crates/*/tests/vectors/. Each one is the
# byte-exact expected output of an encoder, and the golden vector suites
# compare against these bytes on every test run. They are the record of what
# the wire and state encodings actually produce.
#
# RECORDED: 2026-09-07, at commit c2373af (c2373af5f670cc2f1d115af91543c9cbfb80eab7).
#
# WHY: these files are rewritten in place by their own test suites when
# UPDATE_VECTORS=1. Until 3bd6e28 that gate was spelled
# std::env::var("UPDATE_VECTORS").is_ok() in four of the five suites, which is
# true for UPDATE_VECTORS=0 and for the empty string, so any run that merely
# had the variable set overwrote the vectors and passed. This manifest is the
# fixed point taken immediately after that gate was corrected: it is the
# reference for deciding whether a later change to these bytes was intended.
#
# A change to any hash below means an encoding changed. That is a consensus
# level event, not a test fixture update, and it should be explained in the
# commit that causes it rather than absorbed silently.
#
# NOTHING IN THIS REPOSITORY VERIFIES THIS FILE. No test reads it, no CI step
# checks it. It is a record, not a gate. The gate that does exist is
# crates/codec/tests/gate_update_vectors_pin.rs, which checks how the
# regeneration branch is spelled, plus the git diff --exit-code step in
# .github/workflows/ci.yml, which fails a build whose tests modified a tracked
# file. Neither of those compares against the hashes below.
#
# To check by hand, from the repository root:
#   shasum -a 256 -c docs/golden-vectors.sha256
#
